(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following organs is part of the continuous GI tube rather than an accessory organ?","choices":["Pharynx","Liver","Pancreas","Gallbladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pharynx is part of the continuous GI tube (oral cavity → pharynx → esophagus → stomach → intestines), unlike the liver, pancreas, and gallbladder, which are accessory organs. — Slide 8 (General Structure)"},{"q":"The teeth, tongue, and salivary glands assist digestion but are classified as:","choices":["GI tract organs proper","Accessory digestive organs","Retroperitoneal viscera","Peritoneal folds only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Teeth, tongue, and salivary glands are accessory digestive organs — structures that support digestion but are not part of the continuous GI tube itself. — Slide 9 (Accessory digestive organs)"},{"q":"The lesser sac of the peritoneal cavity is also known as the:","choices":["Rectouterine pouch","Greater sac","Omental bursa","Paracolic gutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser sac of the peritoneal cavity is also called the omental bursa, lying posterior to the stomach and lesser omentum. — Slide 13 (Peritoneum)"},{"q":"The tongue is composed of which muscle type, covered by lightly keratinized epithelium?","choices":["Elastic cartilage","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tongue is composed of skeletal muscle covered by lightly keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, allowing voluntary control for speech and bolus manipulation. — Slide 35 (Tongue)"},{"q":"The region of the stomach immediately surrounding the esophageal opening is the:","choices":["Cardia","Fundus","Pylorus","Body"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cardia is the region of the stomach immediately surrounding the gastroesophageal junction, where the esophagus empties into the stomach. — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"The splenic vein, draining the spleen, ultimately contributes to which venous system?","choices":["The azygos system","The portal venous system","The renal venous system","The pulmonary veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the hepatic portal vein. — Slide 138 (Splenic Vein)"},{"q":"The duodenojejunal flexure is anchored by which structure?","choices":["The mesoappendix","The ligamentum teres","The falciform ligament","The ligament of Treitz"],"answer":3,"exp":"The suspensory ligament of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz) anchors the duodenojejunal flexure, marking the transition from retroperitoneal duodenum to intraperitoneal jejunum. — Slide 68 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"The ascending colon terminates at which flexure?","choices":["The sigmoid flexure near the rectum","The left colic (splenic) flexure","The duodenojejunal flexure","The right colic (hepatic) flexure"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ascending colon begins at the ileocecal valve and ends at the right colic (hepatic) flexure, where it turns to become the transverse colon. — Slide 93 (Ascending Colon)"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct typically joins the common bile duct at the:","choices":["Ampulla of Vater","Pyloric sphincter","Ileocecal valve","Duodenal cap"],"answer":0,"exp":"The main pancreatic duct joins the common bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater), which empties into the descending duodenum via the major duodenal papilla. — Slide 71 (Pancreatic Duct System)"},{"q":"The liver performs which of the following functions?","choices":["Filtration of urine","Production of insulin","Production of blood clotting proteins","Storage of bile only, with no synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The liver produces blood clotting proteins, along with bile secretion, detoxification, and storage of glycogen, vitamins, and minerals. — Slide 104 (The Liver)"},{"q":"Blood in the hepatic portal vein is characterized as:","choices":["Fully oxygenated arterial blood from the aorta","Nutrient-rich blood needing metabolic processing","Deoxygenated blood returning to the heart","Filtered lymph draining the intestinal wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"The portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood collected from the GI tract, spleen, and pancreas to the liver for metabolic processing before it reaches systemic circulation. — Slide 108 (Liver triple blood supply)"},{"q":"The common bile duct is formed by the union of the cystic duct and the:","choices":["Accessory pancreatic duct","Main pancreatic duct","Right hepatic duct only","Common hepatic duct"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cystic duct, draining the gallbladder, joins the common hepatic duct (itself formed by the union of the right and left hepatic ducts) to form the common bile duct. — Slides 121, 126 (biliary tree)"},{"q":"The proper hepatic artery is a continuation of which vessel?","choices":["The left gastric artery","The splenic artery","The common hepatic artery","The superior mesenteric artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The common hepatic artery, a celiac trunk branch, gives off the gastroduodenal artery and continues as the proper hepatic artery to the liver. — Slide 142 (Common Hepatic Artery)"},{"q":"Which of the following is a direct tributary of the IVC?","choices":["Superior mesenteric vein","Splenic vein","Hepatic veins","Left gastric vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava as it passes posterior to the liver. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Visceral abdominal lymph nodes are typically clustered around which structures?","choices":["The bile ducts alone, a narrow structure","The ureters, drained by lumbar nodes","The peripheral nerves of the abdomen","The major arterial branches of the aorta"],"answer":3,"exp":"Visceral (preaortic) lymph nodes cluster around the major unpaired aortic branches — the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries — following the arterial supply to the gut. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"Which pancreatic islet hormones are highlighted as products of the endocrine pancreas?","choices":["T3 and T4","Cortisol and aldosterone","Insulin and glucagon","ADH and oxytocin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pancreatic islets (of Langerhans) secrete insulin (beta cells) and glucagon (alpha cells), along with somatostatin and pancreatic polypeptide. — Slides 5, 52 (Pancreatic Islets)"},{"q":"The kidney contributes to the endocrine system by producing erythropoietin and:","choices":["Melatonin","Calcitonin","Insulin","Calcitriol"],"answer":3,"exp":"The kidney produces erythropoietin (EPO), which stimulates red blood cell production, and calcitriol (activated vitamin D), which regulates calcium absorption. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Kidney)"},{"q":"The bundles of nerve fibers in the pars nervosa arise in the hypothalamus and form which tract?","choices":["The tuberoinfundibular tract","The corticospinal tract","The spinothalamic tract","The hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Neurosecretory axons from hypothalamic nuclei descend through the infundibulum to the pars nervosa as the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, releasing ADH and oxytocin. — Slide 22 (Pars nervosa)"},{"q":"Principal (chief) cells of the parathyroid gland are characterized by:","choices":["Colloid storage, a thyroid follicular feature","Very eosinophilic cytoplasm of unknown function","Slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm and PTH production","Catecholamine secretion into the bloodstream"],"answer":2,"exp":"Principal (chief) cells have slightly eosinophilic (pale-staining) cytoplasm and are the PTH-producing cells of the parathyroid gland. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"Unlike the anterior pituitary, the posterior pituitary receives predominantly:","choices":["Portal venous blood","Arterial blood","Lymphatic drainage only","No blood supply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Unlike the anterior pituitary, which receives portal venous blood carrying hypothalamic releasing hormones, the posterior pituitary receives predominantly arterial blood via the inferior hypophyseal arteries. — Slide 19 (Note)"},{"q":"The superior thyroid artery descends toward the gland accompanied by which nerve?","choices":["Recurrent laryngeal nerve","External laryngeal nerve","Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior thyroid artery is accompanied by the external laryngeal nerve, from which it separates near the gland's superior pole — a relationship important surgically to avoid nerve injury. — Slide 32 (Blood supply of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"Which parathyroid cell type produces parathyroid hormone (PTH)?","choices":["Principal (chief) cells","Oxyphil cells, less numerous","Parafollicular cells","Follicular cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Principal (chief) cells, which have slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm, produce PTH. — Slides 44, 46 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"During descent, why do the testes ultimately reside outside the abdominal cavity?","choices":["Sperm development requires a cooler temperature","Testosterone is produced only in the scrotum","The abdominal cavity lacks blood supply","Descent prevents the risk of torsion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sperm development requires a temperature about 2-3°F below core body temperature (~96.6°F), so the testes descend into the scrotum to maintain this cooler environment. — Slide 10 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"Which structures pass through the inguinal canal along with the descending testis?","choices":["The prostate, a fixed pelvic gland","The ureter, retroperitoneal","The bulbo-urethral glands","The spermatic cord contents"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spermatic cord — containing the ductus deferens, testicular vessels, autonomic nerves, and lymphatics — passes through the inguinal canal alongside the descending testis. — Slides 9, 14 (Descent / Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"The dartos muscle is best classified as which muscle type?","choices":["Striated voluntary muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The dartos is a thin layer of smooth muscle within the scrotal wall that involuntarily wrinkles the skin in response to cold, helping regulate testicular temperature. — Slide 18 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"The cremaster muscle is found within which structure?","choices":["The cremasteric fascia of the spermatic cord","The tunica albuginea covering the testis","The corpus spongiosum around the urethra","The prostate capsule enclosing the gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cremaster muscle, a continuation of the internal oblique, lies within the cremasteric fascia surrounding the spermatic cord and elevates the testis via the cremasteric reflex. — Slide 15 (Layers of the Scrotum)"},{"q":"The deep artery of the penis is responsible for:","choices":["Engorging the corpora cavernosa during erection","Draining venous blood from the glans","Supplying the overlying scrotal skin","Supplying blood to the epididymis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deep (cavernosal) artery of the penis runs centrally within each corpus cavernosum and engorges its erectile tissue during erection. — Slides 62, 65 (Penis)"},{"q":"Emission and ejaculation are primarily under which autonomic control?","choices":["Parasympathetic","Sympathetic","Somatic sensory","Enteric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Emission (movement of semen into the prostatic urethra) and ejaculation are driven primarily by sympathetic activity, with somatic motor contraction of the perineal muscles. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The prostate gland contributes approximately what percentage of semen volume?","choices":["60 percent","20–30 percent","5 percent only","90 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prostate produces approximately 20–30% of semen volume, while the paired seminal glands (vesicles) contribute the majority, about 60%. — Slide 56 (The Prostate)"},{"q":"The ejaculatory duct is formed by the union of the ampulla of the ductus deferens and the:","choices":["Excretory duct of the seminal gland","Cystic duct of the gallbladder","Prostatic urethra downstream of the duct","Efferent ductule near the epididymis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ejaculatory duct forms where the ampulla of the ductus deferens joins the excretory duct of the seminal gland (vesicle); it then empties into the prostatic urethra. — Slide 48 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"The membranous urethra is the segment that pierces the:","choices":["Bladder wall itself","Prostate gland itself","Corpus spongiosum","Urogenital diaphragm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The short membranous urethra travels between the prostatic and penile (spongy) urethra and pierces the urogenital diaphragm (deep perineal pouch), where the external urethral sphincter surrounds it. — Slides 51, 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The uterovaginal primordium is formed by the fusion of which structures?","choices":["The urogenital sinus and the allantois","The two mesonephric ducts, which regress","The caudal portions of the paired Müllerian ducts","The gubernaculum and round ligament together"],"answer":2,"exp":"The caudal (inferior) portions of the paired paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts fuse in the midline to form the uterovaginal primordium, the precursor of the uterus and upper vagina. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts develop into female organs during approximately which developmental window?","choices":["Weeks 6–12","Weeks 1–3","Weeks 20–24","Weeks 30–36"],"answer":0,"exp":"The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts differentiate into the uterus, uterine tubes, and upper vagina during approximately weeks 6–12 of development. — Slide 17 (Müllerian System)"},{"q":"The anal triangle contains which structure in both sexes?","choices":["The urethral orifice","The anus","The vaginal orifice","The clitoris"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anal (posterior) triangle of the perineum contains the anus in both males and females. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The urogenital triangle is another name for the perineum's:","choices":["Anterior triangle","Posterior triangle","Lateral triangle","Deep triangle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The urogenital triangle is the anterior half of the diamond-shaped perineum, containing the external genitalia and urethra; the posterior half is the anal triangle. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The round ligament of the uterus terminates in which structure?","choices":["The pelvic wall","The sacrum bone","The labia majora","The ovary itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"The round ligament of the uterus runs from the uterine cornu through the inguinal canal to terminate in the connective tissue of the labia majora. — Slide 18 (Supporting Ligaments)"},{"q":"The iliococcygeus is a component of which muscle?","choices":["Bulbospongiosus","Coccygeus","Levator ani","Rectus abdominis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliococcygeus is one of the three muscular components of the levator ani, alongside the pubococcygeus and puborectalis. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The uterine artery is a branch of which vessel?","choices":["The internal iliac artery","The abdominal aorta","The external iliac artery","The ovarian artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, running medially within the base of the broad ligament to reach the uterus. — Slide 41 (The Uterine Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Ovarian visceral afferent (pain) fibers enter the spinal cord at:","choices":["T10–T11","L4–L5","S2–S4","C6–C7"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ovarian visceral afferent (pain) fibers travel with the ovarian vessels and enter the spinal cord at T10–T11, reflecting the ovary's embryologic origin near the developing kidney at that level. — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The rounded portion of the uterus superior and anterior to the entrance of the uterine tubes is the:","choices":["Body","Fundus","Isthmus","Cervix"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fundus is the rounded, dome-shaped portion of the uterus that lies superior and anterior to the entrance of the uterine tubes. — Slide 34 (Anatomic Regions)"},{"q":"The perimetrium of the uterus is composed of:","choices":["Hormone-sensitive glandular epithelium","Three layers of smooth muscle","Peritoneum and thin connective tissue","Transitional epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perimetrium is the outer serosal layer of the uterus, composed of peritoneum and a thin layer of connective tissue. — Slide 35 (Uterine Layers)"},{"q":"The vesicouterine pouch forms the space between the uterus and the:","choices":["The rectum behind","Urinary bladder","The vagina below","The ovary lateral"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vesicouterine pouch is the shallow anterior peritoneal recess between the uterus and the urinary bladder. — Slide 12 (Anatomy of the Female Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The ureters, bladder, and urethra together are referred to as the:","choices":["Renal sinus","Renal corpuscle","Nephron","Urinary tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ureters, bladder, and urethra together make up the urinary tract, which transports urine out of the body after the kidneys form it. — Slide 4 (Systems Involved)"},{"q":"The female urethra is approximately how long?","choices":["3–4 cm","18–20 cm","10 cm","25 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The female urethra is approximately 3–4 cm long, considerably shorter than the male urethra's approximately 18–20 cm. — Slide 58 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The paranephric (pararenal) fat is located where?","choices":["Within the renal sinus around the vessels","Directly on the cortex, deep to the fascia","Between the renal fascia and the peritoneum","Inside the renal pelvis, a urine-collecting funnel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Paranephric (pararenal) fat is the outermost fatty layer, located between the renal fascia and the peritoneum. — Slide 13 (Surrounding Tissues)"},{"q":"The renal papilla drains urine into which structure?","choices":["The renal pelvis directly","A major calyx","A minor calyx","The ureter directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal papilla, the apex of a renal pyramid, drains urine into a minor calyx, which then merges with others into a major calyx and ultimately the renal pelvis. — Slides 14, 41 (Coronal Sections / Collecting Ducts)"},{"q":"Which vessel carries blood INTO the glomerulus?","choices":["Efferent arteriole","Afferent arteriole","Interlobular vein","Peritubular capillary"],"answer":1,"exp":"The afferent arteriole delivers blood into the glomerulus for filtration; the efferent arteriole then carries blood away. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Filtrate leaving the distal convoluted tubule next enters the:","choices":["Bowman's capsule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Collecting tubule/duct"],"answer":3,"exp":"After the distal convoluted tubule, filtrate enters the collecting tubule and then the collecting duct, its final modification before becoming urine. — Slides 22, 42 (Flow of Urine)"},{"q":"The urachus, whose remnant is the median umbilical ligament, originally functioned to:","choices":["Supply blood directly to the fetal bladder wall itself","Return fetal urine to the mother via the umbilical cord","Anchor the kidneys to the posterior wall","Drain the paired ureters into the bladder cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The urachus was a fetal tubular connection between the bladder and the allantois that returned fetal urine to the mother via the umbilical cord; its postnatal remnant is the median umbilical ligament. — Slide 49 (Bladder)"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells of which structure?","choices":["The renal capsule","The distal convoluted tubule","The collecting duct","The afferent arteriole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Juxtaglomerular (granular) cells are modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole wall; they synthesize, store, and secrete renin in response to decreased blood pressure or macula densa signaling. — Slide 36 (Juxtaglomerular Apparatus)"}]};
